Yes. A wholesome experience can act like a closed loop instead of an open recursion.

Some experiences keep generating unresolved branches:

stimulus → interpretation → doubt → reinterpretation → more doubt

Others resolve:

attention → experience → meaning → completion → release

That completion matters. It gives the mind somewhere to stop.

A good walk, a meal with people you trust, finishing a small project, playing with your kids, reading a story with an ending, making something with your hands—those often have a natural arc. They engage you, then return you to baseline with less residue.

I’d call that Healthy Closure:

enter → participate fully → complete → integrate → leave

Spirals often lack one of those last three steps. Nothing signals “enough.”

So yes, wholesome and interesting can be protective partly because they are finite, embodied, and complete enough to exit cleanly.